{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789564917,"nonce":"28HQPfARGM2ymECzVYfUGiz2rEI=","title":"Tier-0 requests LLVM promotion before a loop demonstrates demand","message":"Cranelift emits __zyntax_osr_request at entry to every tier-0 function with a resumable loop. A short loop can finish in Cranelift before the asynchronous LLVM compile starts. After static callee fan-out was removed in fcc6de341ef8c7bad0afb6f716b0895e9951d8b3, a traced 12-trial nbody_modified run still requested and compiled zb_float_repr after the benchmark timings had printed; the run queued three tier-2 compiles. The call at function entry also provides no evidence that a loop is reached.\n\nCount visits to resumable loop headers in an SSA value local to the invocation. Request promotion on the 1024th visit, once per long-running frame. Keep the helper-slot load at every header so an already-running baseline frame can transfer when a helper lands. The requester call now lives on a rare branch; its impact on register allocation needs kernel measurement. An execution regression test should prove a short loop makes no request and a long loop makes exactly one, resetting on the next call.\n\nWith the change, the 12-trial nbody trace showed advance requesting promotion after becoming hot and being declined by its separate call-heavy gate, and no tier-2 helper compiles. The LLVM aggregate ABI promotion test and the full LLVM-enabled compiler and Python suites passed. Full 50-trial checked speed verdict: float 2.84 ms vs PyPy 7.71 ms, nbody_modified 310.86 ms vs PyPy 7.50 ms, richards 1.09 ms vs PyPy 1.22 ms. These runs do not isolate steady-state speedup from machine variation. A request can still become stale while it waits behind another broker job; a remaining-work check at compile start and an LLVM headroom ceiling remain in dbe58cb228f043aa957061e5bea6f2e52823e1d5e3b4c6e0832b0e709318ad70.","files":null}]}